#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133529 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "e7fb27f0-67b3-58fa-a582-acd4a4ebe045" :question/math-academy-id "e-5588" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n${-2x_{1} - x_{2} = 12; x_{1} - 2x_{2} + x_{3} = 0; - x_{2} - 2x_{3} =-12; x_{1} - x_{3} = 0$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form\n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}Ax^(^) &= A^{T}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(^) &= (A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T}b \\\\\n&= (\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133530 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "9f8a0e56-d0c5-56dd-9204-c13922ec8ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5589" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form\n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}Ax^(^) &= A^{T}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(^) &= (A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T}b \\\\\n&= (\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133586 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "af2b3f92-fed5-5a50-b662-eaadd9b7adbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-7429" :question/problem "Consider the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form\n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(A)\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(b) \\\\\nAx &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}Ax^(^) &= A^{T}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(^) &= (A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T}b \\\\\n&= (\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is attained when\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}={\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}\n$$\n\nwhich is the least-squares solution of the system\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the column space of $A$, and $\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ and the column space.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAx^(^) - b &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}-\\mathbf{b}\\Vert =\\sqrt{(-12)^2+0^2+(-12)^2+0^2}=12\\sqrt{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99df3431-adba-550d-ae72-313470146593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132171} {:answer/id #uuid "cc592562-91ca-5ec5-8f8d-093bd990ff36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132172} {:answer/id #uuid "dac5b559-03eb-51ec-9c9a-3e9e8991a41d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132173} {:answer/id #uuid "08a60458-7aac-5e30-a0de-661c967ff1b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132174} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb75956-c324-5a0d-bbf8-074c7d025a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc592562-91ca-5ec5-8f8d-093bd990ff36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132172} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13ab6eb-3fcb-5f1b-8f86-9be9948e9741" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132170}] :question/id #uuid "72ebe648-c9aa-5496-8f25-29cd029ad4fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-41775" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n${x_{1} + 2x_{2} + x_{3} = 18; x_{1} - 3x_{2} = 0; x_{1} + 2x_{2} - x_{3} = 18; x_{1} - x_{2} = 0$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfd5b6a0-c978-5625-bde0-def409d433d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132189} {:answer/id #uuid "1d77ec79-6dcf-5ad5-b961-37121fa4a294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132190} {:answer/id #uuid "211d052f-94dc-5992-bef1-246d5777db10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132191} {:answer/id #uuid "382ac189-ec0a-51e3-8e71-f1b5a52048b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132192} {:answer/id #uuid "489c370c-f718-51aa-82bb-93dd6e087480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "211d052f-94dc-5992-bef1-246d5777db10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132191} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b64b46-d957-5cf3-b08a-7771bb0e472a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132188}] :question/id #uuid "85fd6f83-baf3-5731-972e-5511c5fc2e85" :question/math-academy-id "q-41783" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax = b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax - b‖$.\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$"}]}
